From ffea9e08279a0b5c4846f3ce7dc22507c3a8ffba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:08:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 08/14] doc: the feature bits this chain uses, and why the
 numbers are wrong

This document reserved 2100/2101 for a unified-signing channel type, and
a later revision moved that to 32769/32768 on Chris Guida's reasoning.
Meanwhile v26.06.7-blake2b.4 shipped option_blake2b as bit 68 and
option_unified_sigs as bit 70, so the question stopped being what the
numbers should be and became which numbers are on the wire.

They are 68 and 70. A number already deployed is the number, whatever it
should have been, and a second implementation that picked different ones
would simply not interoperate. So this describes 68/69 and 70/71, which
is what Lightning Fork implements, and keeps the argument about the
numbering as an argument rather than as a competing assignment.

The argument is still worth making. The highest pair BOLT 9 has assigned
is 66/67, so 68 and 70 are the next ones the spec hands out rather than
spare ones, and a pair in the range implementations already treat as
custom sits clear of that. The cost of moving rises with every channel
opened under the current numbers.

What this document does adopt from Chris's plan is the asymmetry, which
is the part I had missed and which matters more than the range. Odd in
init and node_announcement, because peering wants to be permissive
during a migration and an even bit there refuses more than it means to,
including client applications that speak the wire protocol only to reach
a node's RPC. That is the same breakage found in review of this series
against cln-application. Even inside channel_type, because a channel
type is a set of even bits and both sides must agree on the digest they
sign. Neither in invoices or offers, because the invoice prefix and
chain_hash already refuse a payer from the other chain.
